Pages = {1010-1022},
Month = {MAR 1},
Abstract = {Civil responders currently have limited information available to them to
   support flood incident planning. A new generation of tools are emerging
   that produce more detailed understanding of flood impacts on people and
   accessibility during floods. These are typically applied in isolation,
   proving only a partial assessment of impacts. This paper integrates
   analysis of flood hydraulics, transport accessibility and human safety
   to explore the impact of flooding on pedestrians and drivers, and its
   implications on emergency routes and service areas. A reference
   scenario, developed and used by the local Civil Protection Agency, is
   applied to Galluzzo in Florence (Italy). Results shows that 37\% of
   inhabitants live dose to roads where they can be swept away, and 78\%
   live in locations where parked vehicles can be transported by
   floodwaters. Furthermore, at its worst 22.5\% of road extension is
   inaccessible; and all hospitals, fire and police stations cannot be
   reached, highlighting the need to take preventative action from the
   outset of an event that is predicted to lead to substantial inundation.
   Integration of multiple indicators of flood impacts, especially those
   most relevant to human safety, is fundamental to civil responders if
   they are to successfully planning and implement emergency response
   operations in urban environments. (C) 2018 The Authors. Published by
   Elsevier B.V.},
